<http://gcrc.carleton.ca/ontologies/2023/GCRC_CEBP_Genome_Architecture//Resources>
  a owl:Class ;
  rdfs:subClassOf [
    a owl:Restriction ;
    owl:onProperty <http://gcrc.carleton.ca/ontologies/2023/GCRC_CEBP_Genome_Architecture/hasKeyword> ;
    owl:someValuesFrom <http://gcrc.carleton.ca/ontologies/2023/GCRC_CEBP_Genome_Architecture/ConservationStrategy>
  ], [
    a owl:Restriction ;
    owl:onProperty <http://gcrc.carleton.ca/ontologies/2023/GCRC_CEBP_Genome_Architecture/hasKeyword> ;
    owl:someValuesFrom <http://gcrc.carleton.ca/ontologies/2023/GCRC_CEBP_Genome_Architecture/GenomicsMethod>
  ], [
    a owl:Restriction ;
    owl:onProperty <http://gcrc.carleton.ca/ontologies/2023/GCRC_CEBP_Genome_Architecture/hasKeyword> ;
    owl:someValuesFrom <http://gcrc.carleton.ca/ontologies/2023/GCRC_CEBP_Genome_Architecture/GenomicsTerm>
  ] ;
  rdfs:comment """Note that in the gemknow graph database system uses Neo4j software, which doesn’t allow two properties (e.g., Annotation Properties) to have the same name. If more than one annotation property of the same name is needed, this system  stores them as arrays. This storage solution is JSON-LD compliant. Example:

  "@context": {
    "dcterms": "http://purl.org/dc/terms/"
  },
  "@id": "http://example.org/resource1",
  "dcterms:source": ["Source 1", "Source 2", "Source 3"]
}

The accompanying CYPHER query, for example, would have multiple dcterms:source properties in square brackets, as follows:

CREATE (n:Example {`dcterms:source`:["example1.com", "example2.com"]})""" .

